![Posterior distribution of the change in tissue area between day 7 and day 14 under three models. Dots mark the median, thick and thin bars the 50% and 95% credible intervals.](appendix-assumption-tests_files/figure-html/fig-appendix_change_7_14-1.png){#fig-appendix_change_7_14 fig-alt='Three half-eye plots of the change in tissue area from day 7 to day 14. The two straight-line models, with normal and with Student-t errors, are centered near minus 1.9 square millimeters with narrow intervals. The model with free means and spreads is centered near minus 4.5 with an interval that does not overlap the other two.' width=768}
:::
:::








Two things happened, and a test told us neither. First, relaxing normality did essentially nothing: the Student-t line puts the change at -1.9 mm² (95% interval -2.3 to -1.5), against -1.9 (-2.4 to -1.5) for the normal line. Normality was not carrying this claim, exactly as the Shapiro-Wilk result in the main chapter ($p$ = 0.36) happened to suggest, though for reasons that result could not have told us. Second, relaxing linearity and equal variance changed the answer completely: -4.5 mm² (-5.5 to -3.6), more than twice as much tissue lost, with an interval that does not overlap the line's. The narrow interval of the straight line is not careful. It is confidently wrong, because the line is being asked to describe two stretches of the time course with one slope.

The spread tells the same story. The free model estimates a spread of about 3.2 mm² at day 3 and between 0.7 and 1.2 mm² on the other days, whereas the straight line reports a single 2.2, which is too large for four days and too small for one. That is the picture the residual plots and the posterior predictive checks in the main chapter were pointing at, now expressed as the number the claim depends on.

::: callout-tip
### Frequentist equivalent

The same free model exists on the frequentist side, but not in `parsnip`, which has no engine that models the spread. `glmmTMB` does, through its `dispformula` argument, and it lands on essentially the same change between day 7 and day 14. As with every pair in this book, the two lenses agree when the data carry the estimate; what the Bayesian fit adds here is the full posterior for each day's spread, estimated from as few as 7 animals.








::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
library(glmmTMB)

# One mean and one spread per day by maximum likelihood
free_tmb <- glmmTMB(Tissue_Area ~ DPI_f, dispformula = ~ DPI_f, data = cell_data)

# Day-14 minus day-7 mean and its standard error (variance of a difference)
b <- fixef(free_tmb)$cond
V <- vcov(free_tmb)$cond
est <- unname(b["DPI_f14"] - b["DPI_f7"])
se <- sqrt(V["DPI_f14", "DPI_f14"] + V["DPI_f7", "DPI_f7"] - 2 * V["DPI_f14", "DPI_f7"])
round(c(estimate = est, lower = est - 1.96 * se, upper = est + 1.96 * se), 1)
```

