## What I would write in a methods section instead

Putting the pieces together, here is a paragraph that says what was done and lets a reader judge it, in place of "assumptions were met":

> I examined residuals by day and posterior predictive checks of the mean and standard deviation of tissue area at each day, which showed that a single straight line was wrong on average at two of five days and that a single residual spread was too large for four days and too small for one. The 7-to-14-day change under the straight line was -1.9 mm² (95% credible interval -2.4 to -1.5); under a model with a free mean and spread per day it was -4.5 mm² (-5.5 to -3.6), and I report the latter. Replacing normal errors with Student-t errors changed the straight-line estimate by 0.04 mm², so normality was not carrying the result.

That paragraph contains no p-value and no "assumptions were met", and it tells the reader more than any battery of tests could. It also says something the tests can never say: how much the answer depended on each assumption.
